1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:05,500
Bolna open source has no dashboard. So how do you actually build an agent?

2
00:00:05,630 --> 00:00:08,785
The no-code UI is not part of the repo.

3
00:00:08,915 --> 00:00:10,895
You build agents in Python,

4
00:00:11,025 --> 00:00:20,367
or as JSON, through a small agent API. This episode
walks through both, from the repo's own examples and API docs.

5
00:00:24,617 --> 00:00:33,272
The README's minimal example, simple assistant dot py, builds
an agent in Python, without the local telephony setup.

6
00:00:33,402 --> 00:00:36,467
It starts with an assistant, named demo agent.

7
00:00:36,597 --> 00:00:41,071
Then a transcriber: Deepgram, model nova-2, streaming, in English.

8
00:00:41,201 --> 00:00:52,804
Then the LLM agent: a simple LLM agent with
a streaming flow, using OpenAI's gpt-4o-mini, at temperature 0.3.

9
00:00:53,454 --> 00:01:02,574
The synthesizer is ElevenLabs, with the voice George,
on the eleven turbo v2.5 model, streaming WAV audio.

10
00:01:02,704 --> 00:01:15,426
Add task wires all three into one conversation task. The README calls
it a single coherent pipeline: transcriber, LLM, synthesizer. Textual input is off.

11
00:01:15,556 --> 00:01:22,875
And assistant dot execute is an async generator. You
loop over it, and handle each chunk as it streams.

12
00:01:23,525 --> 00:01:26,618
Running it takes three keys, one per provider.

13
00:01:26,748 --> 00:01:28,889
OpenAI, for the LLM,

14
00:01:29,019 --> 00:01:30,935
Deepgram, for transcription,

15
00:01:31,065 --> 00:01:33,029
and ElevenLabs, for the voice.

16
00:01:33,159 --> 00:01:45,192
Then run the script. The README says the exact keys in each chunk depend on
your configured tools and providers. So treat the output as a stream, and process it incrementally.

17
00:01:45,842 --> 00:01:50,724
Want to test the conversation without audio? There is a text-only example.

18
00:01:50,854 --> 00:01:56,447
It drops the transcriber and the synthesizer, and keeps only the LLM agent.

19
00:01:56,577 --> 00:01:59,166
It sets enable textual input to true.

20
00:01:59,296 --> 00:02:01,738
So it needs only your OpenAI key.

21
00:02:01,868 --> 00:02:09,265
Text only assistant dot py is a quick way
to iterate on the conversation logic, before you add voice.

22
00:02:09,915 --> 00:02:17,847
Over the API, an agent is one JSON document.
Here is the example from API dot md, trimmed.

23
00:02:17,977 --> 00:02:22,110
The agent config has a name, Alfred here, and an agent type.

24
00:02:22,240 --> 00:02:30,507
Each task has a toolchain. Execution is parallel,
and the pipeline lists transcriber, LLM, and synthesizer.

25
00:02:30,637 --> 00:02:44,374
Tools config picks the provider for each piece: Twilio for input and output,
in WAV, a Deepgram transcriber, the OpenAI LLM agent, and the ElevenLabs synthesizer.

26
00:02:44,504 --> 00:02:52,624
The task config holds settings like hang up after silence,
30 in the example. And the agent has a welcome message.

27
00:02:52,754 --> 00:02:58,185
Prompts sit beside the config, keyed by task. Task one gets the system prompt.

28
00:02:58,835 --> 00:03:09,995
API dot md documents five endpoints for agents. In the Docker
setup, the Bolna server creates and handles agents, on port 5001.

29
00:03:10,125 --> 00:03:16,153
Post slash agent creates one, and returns its agent ID, with the state created.

30
00:03:16,283 --> 00:03:19,588
Get slash agent, slash the ID, reads it back.

31
00:03:19,718 --> 00:03:24,034
Put on the same path updates it. The body is the same as create.

32
00:03:24,164 --> 00:03:27,549
Delete removes it, and returns the state deleted.

33
00:03:27,679 --> 00:03:32,106
And get slash all lists every agent, with its config and prompts.

34
00:03:32,756 --> 00:03:40,490
No phone yet? The local setup folder also has a
quickstart client, that talks to an agent from your microphone.

35
00:03:40,620 --> 00:03:44,461
It records microphone audio at 16000 hertz.

36
00:03:44,591 --> 00:04:00,847
Each frame is base64 encoded, and sent as an audio message over a websocket, to the chat
endpoint for your agent, on localhost port 5001. The agent ID comes from an assistant ID environment variable.

37
00:04:00,977 --> 00:04:03,100
The agent's audio streams back.

38
00:04:03,230 --> 00:04:09,262
When you interrupt, the server sends a clear
message, and the client drops the audio still queued.

39
00:04:09,392 --> 00:04:16,411
The rest plays through your speakers. If a Bolna API
key is set, the client sends it as a bearer header.

40
00:04:17,061 --> 00:04:18,692
So there are three ways in.

41
00:04:18,822 --> 00:04:22,388
The Python assistant, for building and testing in code.

42
00:04:22,518 --> 00:04:29,232
The agent API, when agents are JSON documents you create and manage over HTTP.

43
00:04:29,362 --> 00:04:35,760
And the Docker setup from the last episode, when
you need real phone calls through Twilio or Plivo.

44
00:04:36,410 --> 00:04:46,741
For more agent configurations, the README links example agents at examples
dot bolna dot dev, to create, use, and start making calls with.

45
00:04:46,871 --> 00:04:54,625
And if you want to contribute a larger change, the project asks
you to open an issue first, and discuss it before you build.

46
00:04:55,275 --> 00:04:56,373
Quick recap.

47
00:04:56,503 --> 00:05:00,972
Simple assistant dot py runs a full voice pipeline in Python.

48
00:05:01,102 --> 00:05:06,609
The text-only example tests the LLM side, with just an OpenAI key.

49
00:05:06,739 --> 00:05:11,606
Over the API, an agent is JSON: a toolchain, and tools config.

50
00:05:11,736 --> 00:05:15,987
Five endpoints create, read, update, delete, and list agents.

51
00:05:16,117 --> 00:05:19,888
And the quickstart client talks to an agent from your microphone.

52
00:05:20,018 --> 00:05:24,751
That completes the open source module of the Bolna AI Masterclass.

53
00:05:24,881 --> 00:05:29,999
This masterclass is created by Omkar Ghate. Go build something great.
